        "titulo": "The present of -ar verbs",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: the infinitive of this verb group ends in «-ar»: «hablar», «trabajar». Drop the «-ar» and you get the stem: «habl-», «trabaj-». STEP 2: add the present-tense endings to the stem: -o, -as, -a, -amos, -áis, -an. «Hablo, hablas, habla, hablamos, habláis, hablan.» STEP 3: the stress falls on the stem, except in «nosotros» and «vosotros», where it falls on the ending: «HAblo», but «ha-BLA-mos», «ha-BLÁ-is». STEP 4: the subject pronoun is almost always left out: «Hablo español», not «Yo hablo español», except for contrast. STEP 5: «usted» takes the same form as «él», and «ustedes» the same as «ellos»: «Usted trabaja», «Ustedes trabajan». «Vosotros» (Spain) carries an accent: «habláis». STEP 6: the present tense covers habits, what's happening right now, and also the near future: «Trabajo en Madrid», «Mañana viajo a Sevilla». STEP 7: watch the pronunciation: the «n» of «hablan» is fully pronounced, with no nasal vowel the way some languages do it; the «j» of «trabajo» is a rough, throaty h (trah-BAH-ho). STEP 8: some -ar verbs change the stem vowel (pensar → pienso, contar → cuento). They come up in later lessons; the ones in this lesson are all regular.",
        "comparacao": "English present tense doesn't change by person the way Spanish does (you say «I speak», «you speak», «they speak» — only «he/she speaks» gets an «-s»), so this full set of six endings has no direct English match. Learn «-amos» (we) and «-áis» (you all, Spain) as new endings rather than translating from English.",
